<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Khwarizmi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 780–c. 850</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Khwarizmi was a Persian mathematician, astronomer and geographer who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in the ninth century. His treatise on calculation by completion and balancing gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> its name, and the word algorithm derives from his name. His geographical work also recorded information about lands far beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, which is why he is cited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>'s profile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi.jpg" alt="al-Khwarizmi" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zarateman · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madrid_-_Ciudad_Universitaria,_Monumento_a_Muhammad_al-Juarismi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The word 'algebra' comes from al-jabr in the title of <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>'s treatise on calculation, written in Abbasid Baghdad around 820.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Abbasid caliphate and after, scholars working in Arabic preserved and translated Greek philosophy and science and made advances of their own in mathematics, with <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>’s algebra; in astronomy, optics and medicine, with Ibn al-Haytham and Ibn Sina; and in history and social thought, with Ibn Khaldun.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>English words such as algebra, alcohol, sugar, coffee, cotton and zero come from Arabic, and algorithm comes from the name of the 9th-century Baghdad mathematician <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The mathematician <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>, working in Baghdad in the early ninth century, wrote a treatise explaining the Indian decimal numerals and another on the methods of solving equations, whose title gave Europe the word “algebra”.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=825" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of al-Khwarizmi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=825">What was here in 825 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…has encouraged me to compose a short work on Calculating by (the rules of) Completion and Reduction, confining it to what is easiest and most useful in arithmetic, such as men constantly require in cases of inheritance, legacies, partition, law-suits, and trade…&#8221;</p><footer>The Algebra, author's preface &#183; tr. Frederic Rosen, 1831 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Compendious_Book_on_Calculation_by_Completion_and_Balancing/The_author%E2%80%99s_preface" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The learned in times which have passed away, and among nations which have ceased to exist, were constantly employed in writing books on the several departments of science and on the various branches of knowledge…&#8221;</p><footer>The Algebra, author's preface &#183; tr. Frederic Rosen, 1831 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Compendious_Book_on_Calculation_by_Completion_and_Balancing/The_author%E2%80%99s_preface" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
